    "summary": "US Ambassador to Ethiopia Ervin J. Massinga says Washington's policy on Somalia remains unchanged, following President Trump's comments that some interpreted as differentiating Somaliland from the rest of Somalia.",
    "content": "The United States Ambassador to Ethiopia, Ervin J. Massinga, stated that there has been no alteration in American foreign policy regarding Somalia. His remarks came in response to questions about recent comments by President Donald Trump that some observers interpreted as drawing a distinction between Somaliland and the rest of Somalia. Massinga spoke in an interview with The Reporter, an Ethiopian newspaper.\n\nIn the interview, Massinga was asked about the implications of Trump's statements for the US government's position on Somaliland. The ambassador clarified that the president's remarks do not signify any shift in policy. He emphasized that the US maintains its existing stance on Somalia and that there has been no change in the approach to the region.\n\nThe clarification comes amid heightened attention to the Horn of Africa, where the status of Somaliland has been a sensitive topic. Somaliland declared independence from Somalia in 1991 but has not received widespread international recognition. The United States, like most countries, does not formally recognize Somaliland as an independent state and continues to support Somalia's territorial integrity.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the ambassador's statement may ease concerns about potential shifts in US policy that could affect Somalia's sovereignty. Many diaspora communities are closely watching international developments related to Somaliland, as any change in recognition could have significant implications for families, investments, and political dynamics back home. The US is home to a large Somali diaspora, particularly in Minnesota, which remains engaged in political and economic developments in the region.\n\nMassinga's remarks underscore the importance of clear communication from US officials on foreign policy matters, especially in regions where diaspora communities are invested. The interview with The Reporter aimed to address speculation and provide clarity on the US position, which remains consistent with previous statements.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/20/mareykanka-oo-sheegay-inaan-siyaasaddii-ku-saabsan-soomaaliya-waxba-iskama-beddelin/",
